# Exact bytes, always.
#
# Why this file exists, measured: `git init` on Windows turns on
# core.autocrlf=true. With that, git STORES a file with LF and HANDS IT BACK
# with CRLF. The reversal proof caught it immediately — a script stored at 1,356
# bytes came back at 1,394, a difference of exactly 38 bytes for 38 lines. On a
# corpus of 2,615 files, 1,003 were affected.
#
# This is a safety backup, not a collaborative code repository. The only thing it
# promises is to hand the file back EXACTLY as it was. A backup that alters what
# it returns is not a backup — and the alteration is invisible: the file still
# works, `git status` is permanently dirty, and nobody suspects anything.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions.
* -text

*.pkl binary
*.pyc binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
